VACUUM can only remove a dead version once **no snapshot can still see it**. Anything that holds an old snapshot holds back cleanup for the whole database:

- a long-running or **idle in transaction** session (check `pg_stat_activity.state`, `xact_start`, `backend_xmin`);
- an old **prepared transaction** (`pg_prepared_xacts`);
- a stale **replication slot** (`pg_replication_slots`).

Set `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` (per role or session) so abandoned transactions do not block cleanup. Running `VACUUM FULL` does not help here: it cannot discard versions an old snapshot can still see either.

A `SECURITY DEFINER` function runs with the privileges of its owner, which makes it a privilege-escalation surface. The docs' safe pattern:

```sql
BEGIN;
CREATE FUNCTION admin.check_password(uname text, pass text)
RETURNS boolean
LANGUAGE sql
SECURITY DEFINER
SET search_path = admin, pg_temp
AS $$ SELECT pass = password FROM admin.pwds WHERE username = uname $$;
REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION admin.check_password(text, text) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION admin.check_password(text, text) TO admins;
COMMIT;
```

- Pin `search_path` and put `pg_temp` **last**, so a caller cannot plant a temporary object that shadows yours.
- `EXECUTE` is granted to `PUBLIC` by default; revoke it **in the same transaction** as the CREATE so there is no window where anyone can call it.
